  "content": {
    "id": "p146-task-3-question",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{4-x}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p146-task-3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $f(-5)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$3$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-5)&=\\sqrt{4-(-5)}\\\\f(-5)&=\\sqrt{9}\\\\f(-5)&=3\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p146-task-3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "State the domain and range of $f$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Domain: $(-\\infty,4]$.\n\nRange: $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "The radicand must be non-negative:\n$$\\begin{align*}4-x&\\ge 0\\\\4&\\ge x\\\\x&\\le 4.\\end{align*}$$\nHence the domain is $(-\\infty,4]$.\n\nSquare-root outputs are non-negative. The output is $0$ at $x=4$ and increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Hence the range is $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p146-task-3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Decreasing square-root curve with included endpoint $(4,0)$ and $y$-intercept $(0,2)$, extending upwards to the left.",
